Symptoms

Affected devices may exhibit symptoms such as:

Enterprise Wi-Fi fails to authenticate.
Wired 802.1X authentication fails.
The device remains unauthenticated on the network.
VPN connections relying on MS-CHAPv2 authentication fail.
PEAP-MSCHAPv2 authentication stops working after the update.
Removing KB5127648 appears to restore connectivity.

Community reports on Microsoft Q&A describe failures in WPA2-Enterprise/802.1X PEAP environments immediately after installing KB5127648, including OneX Held State timeout behavior.

However, uninstalling the security update should not be the preferred long-term remediation.

Root Cause

The behavior is associated with Microsoft's ongoing work to remove dependencies on NTLMv1-derived credentials.

Before the October 2026 security update, if the following registry value was not configured:

Plain Text
HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Control\Lsa\MSV1_0
BlockNtlmv1SSO

Windows effectively operated in:

Plain Text
Audit mode = 0

Beginning with Windows security updates released on or after October 13, 2026, an unconfigured BlockNtlmv1SSO changes to an internal default of:

Plain Text
Enforce mode = 1

Microsoft states that this change applies when:

Credential Guard is disabled, and
BlockNtlmv1SSO has not been explicitly configured.

It can affect Wi-Fi, Ethernet, and VPN connections using MS-CHAPv2-based authentication.

Before October 2026
Plain Text
BlockNtlmv1SSO missing
|
v
Audit mode (0)
|
v
NTLMv1-derived SSO dependency detected
but authentication permitted
October 2026 and later
Plain Text
BlockNtlmv1SSO missing
|
v
Enforce mode (1)
|
v
NTLMv1-derived SSO credentials blocked
|
v
MS-CHAPv2 / PEAP authentication may fail

This is therefore better understood as an intentional security-hardening change exposing a legacy authentication dependency, rather than simply a defective Windows networking patch.

Important: Credential Guard

Microsoft explicitly states that this particular change affects devices where Credential Guard is disabled.

If Windows Credential Guard is enabled, the BlockNtlmv1SSO behavioral change described here does not take effect.

This distinction should be considered when determining whether a connectivity failure after KB5127648 has the same root cause.

Confirming the Issue
1. Check KB5127648

Run:

PowerShell
Get-HotFix -Id KB5127648

If installed, output should identify:

Plain Text
KB5127648

You can also check:

BAT
wmic qfe | find "KB5127648"
 

The presence of KB5127648 alone does not prove that a network failure is caused by the NTLMv1/MS-CHAPv2 change.

2. Check BlockNtlmv1SSO

Run from an elevated Command Prompt:

BAT
reg query "HKLM\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Control\Lsa\MSV1_0" /v BlockNtlmv1SSO

Or PowerShell:

PowerShell
Get-ItemProperty `
-Path "HKLM:\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Control\Lsa\MSV1_0" `
-Name "BlockNtlmv1SSO" `
-ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
 
Interpretation
Configuration	BehaviorValue missing after October 2026 update	Enforce behavior
BlockNtlmv1SSO = 0	Audit
BlockNtlmv1SSO = 1	Enforce

Microsoft documents the transition from Audit to Enforce as the default when the value isn't configured.

Event Log Validation

For a stronger confirmation, examine:

Plain Text
Event Viewer
> Applications and Services Logs
> Microsoft
> Windows
> NTLM
> Operational

Microsoft guidance identifies:

Plain Text
Event ID 4024

as an audit event indicating the dependency was detected but permitted, while:

Plain Text
Event ID 4025

indicates NTLMv1-derived SSO credentials were blocked.

This provides a much better basis for diagnosis than assuming every connectivity problem after KB5127648 has the same cause.

Temporary Mitigation

For confirmed affected environments that still depend on MS-CHAPv2/PEAP authentication, explicitly configure BlockNtlmv1SSO in Audit mode.

Registry configuration
Plain Text
Registry Path:
HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Control\Lsa\MSV1_0
 
Value Name:
BlockNtlmv1SSO
 
Type:
REG_DWORD
 
Value:
0
Command Prompt

Run as Administrator:

BAT
reg add "HKLM\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Control\Lsa\MSV1_0" /v BlockNtlmv1SSO /t REG_DWORD /d 0 /f
PowerShell
PowerShell
$RegistryPath = "HKLM:\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Control\Lsa\MSV1_0"
 
New-ItemProperty `
-Path $RegistryPath `
-Name "BlockNtlmv1SSO" `
-PropertyType DWord `
-Value 0 `
-Force

Microsoft documents setting BlockNtlmv1SSO to 0 as the temporary Audit-mode mitigation for confirmed authentication failures.

Verify the Fix

After applying the registry configuration:

BAT
reg query "HKLM\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Control\Lsa\MSV1_0" /v BlockNtlmv1SSO

Expected:

Plain Text
BlockNtlmv1SSO REG_DWORD 0x0
 

Or use PowerShell:

PowerShell
$Value = Get-ItemPropertyValue `
-Path "HKLM:\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Control\Lsa\MSV1_0" `
-Name "BlockNtlmv1SSO"
 
Write-Host "BlockNtlmv1SSO = $Value"

Expected:

Plain Text
BlockNtlmv1SSO = 0

Microsoft guidance indicates a Windows restart is not required for this registry change. The affected Wi-Fi, Ethernet, or VPN connection can be disconnected and reconnected to retest authentication.

